 - Seven shader tests need a D3D12 device with shader model 6.6. The Wine build has no D3D12 that works on Metal, so this is the GPU gap that D3DMetal has to fill.
 - The xtask policy test starts git from inside Wine. Run `cargo xtask policy` natively instead.
 
+## Scripted controller input
+
+Set `VICTORY_KEYS_FILE` to a new, empty file before starting a test run. The runtime reads lines appended after GameInput starts. Under Wine, use the file's Windows path.
+
+```sh
+input=$(mktemp /tmp/scarlettmac-input.XXXXXX)
+printf 'Input queue: %s\n' "$input"
+VICTORY_KEYS_FILE="Z:$input" tools/macos/play-main-mac.sh "$game_exe"
+```
+
+After the test reaches the scene, append commands from another terminal. Set `input` there to the path printed by the first command.
+
+```sh
+printf 'W 3\nJ 2 4\nL 2 7\n' >> "$input"
+```
+
+Each line is `key [hold seconds [delay seconds]]`. A key alone taps for 0.2 seconds. `W/A/S/D` move, `I/J/K/L` move the camera, and `Enter` presses A. Holding opposite directions returns that axis to neutral. Append `release` to cancel every queued and active file command. Console controls and physical controllers still work during the test.
+
 ## D3DMetal results, 24 November 2026
 
 With the GPTK cask (`brew install --cask gcenx/wine/game-porting-toolkit`, Wine 7.7 with the D3DMetal of GPTK 1.1), VIctory gets a D3D12 device on Metal, and all 69 shader tests pass on an M4. Metal's sin and cos return 0 for NaN and infinity, where the console GPU returns NaN, so the translator now passes that NaN through. D3DMetal does not check the DXIL container hash.
